    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Palenque was a Maya <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> whose known dynasty began with Kʼuk Balam in 431, and which was sacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calakmul">Calakmul</a> and its client states in 599 and 611 before it recovered.</li><li>Kʼinich Janaabʼ <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinich-janaab-pakal">Pakal</a> ruled from 615 to 683 and, with his sons, built most of the palaces and temples that stand today; the city was sacked again by Toniná in 711.</li><li>Elite building at the ceremonial centre ended some time after 800, farmers stayed for a few generations, and the forest then grew over the site, which was visited, drawn and excavated by outsiders from 1773 onwards.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinich-janaab-pakal">Pakal</a>'s tomb was entered in 1952 by Alberto Ruz Lhuillier, the first person to look on it in over a thousand years, after four years of work at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-the-inscriptions">Temple of the Inscriptions</a>; a jade mosaic mask lay over the king's face.</p></aside>
    </section>

